                    { q: "Is Phuket bad in the rainy season?", a: "Not bad, but different. May–October brings daily rain in bursts (rarely all day) and — more importantly — **rough seas and rip currents on the west-coast beaches**. Island tours can be cancelled. In exchange, hotels are 40–60% cheaper and the island is quiet. Sheltered Phang Nga Bay tours usually still run." },
                    { q: "When is the sea calmest in Phuket?", a: "**December through March**, with January and February the flattest. Underwater visibility peaks at 15–25 metres in this window, which is why serious snorkelling and diving trips concentrate here. The Similan Islands National Park is only open mid-October to mid-May." },
                    { q: "What do red flags on Phuket beaches mean?", a: "**Do not enter the water.** Red flags signal dangerous rip currents, most common May–October. Rips look deceptively calm — a smooth channel running out through the surf. Drownings happen every green season, almost always to swimmers who ignored the flags on an empty-looking beach." },
                    { q: "When is Phuket cheapest?", a: "**May to September** — hotel rates drop 40–60% below peak, five-star resorts go for four-star prices, and last-minute booking works. September and October are the absolute cheapest weeks, and they coincide with the spectacular Phuket Vegetarian Festival." },
                    { q: "How far ahead should I book for Christmas and New Year?", a: "Three to four months. The December 20 – January 10 window is Phuket's most expensive fortnight, with rates 2–3× the annual average, compulsory resort gala dinners and minimum-stay requirements. Island tours over these dates also sell out well in advance." }
