                                { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why is elephant riding bad?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "An elephant's spine isn't built to carry weight — riding chairs cause chronic spinal damage. Any elephant that accepts a rider has been through phajaan, a breaking process using confinement, sleep deprivation and bullhooks." } },
                                { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much does a Phuket elephant sanctuary visit cost?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Roughly ฿2,500–3,500 ($70–100) for a half-day visit including transfer and a meal. Be suspicious of much cheaper offers — adult elephants eat 150–200 kg a day and are expensive to keep." } },
                                { "@type": "Question", "name": "Should I bathe with elephants?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Increasingly, welfare organisations say no. Venues often run a dozen bathing groups per elephant per day, which is stressful. Best-practice sanctuaries are moving toward observation-only visits with feeding as the single point of contact." } }
