                        content: "**Morning — choose your market.** On a **weekend**, this is non-negotiable: Chatuchak, the world's largest weekend market — 15,000 stalls across 27 sections. Go at 9 AM before the heat; see our [Chatuchak survival guide](/thailand/bangkok/chatuchak-weekend-market). On a **weekday**, do the classic combo day trip instead: the [Damnoen Saduak floating market and Maeklong railway market](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-floating-market-railway-market-day-trip-boat-ride) — touristy, yes, but the train squeezing through the folded-back market stalls remains genuinely astonishing.\n\n**2:00 PM — Thonburi canals.** Bangkok was once \"the Venice of the East,\" and the khlongs (canals) of Thonburi are where that city survives. A [longtail boat canal tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-canal-tour) glides past stilt houses, canal-side temples and monitor lizards. Alternatively, explore the same area by bicycle on a [small-group bike tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-temples-canals-local-life-bike-tour) through the green backstreets.\n\n**6:30 PM — Chinatown (Yaowarat).** Bangkok's oldest neighbourhood becomes its greatest food street after dark. Michelin-listed hawkers, charcoal woks, bird's-nest shops, neon Chinese signage — read our full [Yaowarat guide](/thailand/bangkok/chinatown-yaowarat-bangkok), or let a local expert order for you on a [15-tasting Chinatown food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-chinatown-food-tour-15-tastings-michelin-stops). Come hungry: dinner here is eight small meals, not one big one.",
