                        content: "**The train (the romantic option)** — Ordinary and rapid trains leave Hua Lamphong/Bang Sue roughly hourly from early morning; the trip takes 1.5–2 hours and third-class costs from ฿20 — one of the world's great transport bargains. The catch: Ayutthaya station is across the river from the ruins (ferry ฿7), and once there you'll need to hire a tuk-tuk (฿300–400/hour) or rent a bicycle (฿50/day) to cover the spread-out site. Realistic door-to-door: 3+ hours each way with connections.\n\n**Minivans** — From Mo Chit, faster (about 1.5 hours) and cheap (฿70–100), but you still face the same on-arrival transport problem.\n\n**Guided day tour (the efficient option)** — Hotel pickup, air-conditioned transport, a licensed guide who turns brick mounds back into palaces, temple entries, and lunch — compressed into roughly 7 hours door to door. For most visitors this is the right call: our [Ayutthaya ancient temples day trip](/thailand/bangkok/ayutthaya-ancient-temples-day-trip-bangkok-thai-lunch) runs the essential circuit with Thai lunch included from $27 — less than you'd pay for a day of private tuk-tuk hire alone.\n\n**The premium variant** — Combine the ruins with **Bang Pa-In Summer Palace** (the Thai kings' eclectic riverside retreat, 20 minutes south) and return to Bangkok by **river cruise** on the Chao Phraya — the classic \"ruins + palace + boat\" combination on our [Ayutthaya, Bang Pa-In & river cruise day trip](/thailand/bangkok/bang-pa-in-royal-palace-boat-tour).",
