                        title: "Beyond the Food: Golden Buddha, Shrines & Sampeng Lane",
                        content: "**Wat Traimit — the Golden Buddha** (8 AM–5 PM, ฿100) — At Chinatown's eastern gate sits the world's largest solid-gold statue: a 5.5-tonne, 3-metre seated Buddha cast in the Sukhothai era. Its story is barely believable: for two centuries the statue was covered in plaster — a disguise, historians believe, against Burmese invaders — and treated as an ordinary image until 1955, when it slipped from a crane during a move and the plaster cracked open to reveal solid gold beneath. Come early; it's a compact visit of 30–45 minutes.\n\n**Wat Mangkon Kamalawat** — The district's spiritual engine room: Bangkok's most important Chinese Buddhist temple, dense with incense smoke, gilded altars and a steady traffic of devotees. Free; dress respectfully. During Chinese New Year and the Vegetarian Festival this is the celebration's epicentre.\n\n**Sampeng Lane (Soi Wanit 1)** — The district's original market artery: a barely-two-metre-wide canyon of wholesale fabric, beads, toys, stationery and festival goods running parallel to Yaowarat. Go before noon, walk it end to end, and you'll understand how the neighbourhood actually makes its living.\n\n**Talat Kao (Old Market)** — Two hundred years old and still the morning larder: dried seafood, preserved plums, roast duck, live fish. Peak atmosphere before 9 AM.\n\n**The gold shops** — Thailand trades gold in *baht weight* (15.24 g), and Yaowarat's dealers — Hua Seng Heng and its rivals — are where the country comes to buy. The red-and-gold shopfronts with armed guards and electronic price boards are a spectacle in themselves."
